course: dictionary
lesson: 12893
title: A medical word for a nosebleed
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Epistaxis is a long medical word for a nosebleed, which means bleeding from the nose. Doctors, nurses and first-aid books use it, while most people say nosebleed.
explain: «The nurse said Tom had epistaxis after he bumped his nose.» The word looks hard, but the clues are bumped his nose and the blood. Ask: what word would fit here? Nosebleed fits, so epistaxis must mean nosebleed.
explain: Words like this often come from medicine. When you see a long word in a leaflet or a hospital sign, look for the everyday words nearby, and check whether your meaning still makes sense in the sentence.
fact: Epistaxis means a nosebleed: bleeding from the nose.
fact: «Epistaxis» is a formal medical word; everyday people say «nosebleed».
fact: For epistaxis, sit up and lean forward, then pinch the soft part of the nose for about ten minutes.
fact: Breathe through your mouth while you pinch the nose.
fact: Do not tip your head back when you have epistaxis.
exercise: «Sam had epistaxis after he bumped his nose on the goalpost.» What does epistaxis mean? | A bad cold | A broken arm | *A nosebleed | A sore throat
exercise: The first-aid leaflet says to sit up and lean forward during epistaxis. What should you do with your head? | Hold it still and look at the ceiling | Lie flat on the floor | *Lean forward | Tip it right back
exercise: Mia's nose is bleeding. Which step is right? | Tip her head back | Put her to bed flat on her back | *Pinch the soft part of her nose and breathe through her mouth | Run to the shop for a drink
test: «The doctor wrote epistaxis on Ben's notes, because his nose had bled for twenty minutes.» What does epistaxis mean here? | A cough | A sprained ankle | *Bleeding from the nose | A toothache
test: Which is NOT a first-aid step for epistaxis? | Pinch the soft part of the nose | *Tip your head back | Lean forward | Sit up
exam: Nadia says epistaxis is a silly word and everyone should just say nosebleed. Why might a doctor write epistaxis? | *It is the formal medical word for a nosebleed | It means a sore knee | It is the name of a cake | It means nothing at all
